# 008 · Community files and contributor docs

**Status:** `VERIFIED` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Tester basics

## What

Add SECURITY.md (through GitHub's private vulnerability reporting), CONTRIBUTING.md, a code of conduct, issue templates and a pull request template. The bug report asks for the OS, the container engine, the compose tool, the rig-check results and the logs. Rewrite AGENTS.md and CONVENTIONS.md so every rule an outside contributor needs is public. Make the contributor part of `docs/local-development.md` the contributor guide. Its operator tasks move into the docs site with goal [060](060-docs-site.goal).

## Why

Outside testers need a place to report problems and a way to send changes. Today the contributor docs send people to private files.

## Done when

- [x] Each file exists, and GitHub shows the security policy and the templates.
- [x] No public document tells a contributor to read a private file.
- [x] The contributor guide is in `docs/`, and the docs index links it.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-19, owner: The community files ship with v0.1.0.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The terms, as recommended: no contributor agreement, no sign-off rule, and the Contributor Covenant as the code of conduct.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-19: The operator half of `docs/local-development.md` moved to goal [060](060-docs-site.goal), the documentation site.
- 2026-10-20: The files exist, on the owner's terms. `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md` adopts the Contributor Covenant 2.1 by reference, with a short summary, and takes conduct reports privately through GitHub's private vulnerability reporting form, titled "Conduct report:". `SECURITY.md` supports only the latest 0.x release, takes reports through the same private form, and says what counts, what to include and what happens next. `CONTRIBUTING.md` has the terms (no contributor agreement, no sign-off rule), how to send a change, the checks and the rules, and links the site's Contributing page. Under `.github/`: a bug report form (it asks for the OS, the container engine, the compose tool, where the rig runs, the rig-check results and the logs), a feature request form, a chooser that sends security reports to the private form and questions to Discord, and a pull request template.
- 2026-10-20: `AGENTS.md` says that outside contributors don't need the maintainers' private overlay, that deployment-specific logs stay in `.internal/` in your own checkout, and that security problems are reported privately; its push rule covers a contributor's fork. `CONVENTIONS.md` names `CONTRIBUTING.md` as the contributor's entry point. No public document tells a contributor to read a private file.
- 2026-10-20: `docs/local-development.md` is now the contributor guide; the docs index, the README and the site's Contributing page link it under that name. The site's Contributing and Getting help pages point to the new files.
- 2026-10-20: Left: GitHub shows the security policy and the templates only from the default branch, which gets the code in goal [026](026-ship-v0.1.0.goal), and the private reporting form needs private vulnerability reporting on, in goal [002](002-ci-and-repository-settings.goal). The first box is ticked when both are done.
- 2026-10-21: The files are on `main`. GitHub shows them: the community profile is at 100% (README, license, code of conduct, contributing guide, pull request template); the security policy is enabled and linked (`/security/policy`); the new-issue page offers the Bug report and Feature request forms, Report a security vulnerability, and the contact links (a security problem, privately; questions on Discord). Every box is ticked.

